    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Have humans left an indelible "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "mark",
            "ref": "v-mark"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " on Planet Earth? This question seems simple. However, it has led to a battle among scientists. They are arguing about what to call the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "epoch",
            "zh": "纪元"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " we now live in."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "Officially",
            "ref": "v-officially"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", we are in the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Holocene",
            "zh": "全新世"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". This "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "geologic",
            "zh": "地质的"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " epoch began 11,700 years ago, after the last major ice age. But some scientists argue that this label is outdated. They believe we are in the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Anthropocene",
            "zh": "人类世"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "—the “Human Age”—because humans have had many lasting influences on Earth. For example, we have caused many plants and animals to go extinct. We’ve also changed the atmosphere."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The word “Anthropocene” was created in 2000. It’s made up of the Greek words "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "anthropo"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", meaning “man”, and "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "cene"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", meaning “new”."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Many "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "stratigraphers",
            "zh": "地层学家"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " disagree. They say there’s simply no clear proof of a new epoch.“To introduce a new geologic time "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "term",
            "ref": "v-term"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", you need to make it clear where it appears in the rock layers,” says American stratigrapher Whitney Autin. For Autin, the Anthropocene is more about pop culture than hard science. The key question is when exactly humans began to shape the Earth. The use of "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "atomic bombs",
            "zh": "原子弹"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " in the 1940s left radioactive "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "particles",
            "zh": "放射性颗粒"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " in soils worldwide. But deeper in the rock layers, signs of agricultural activity can be found much further back."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Supporters of the Anthropocene admit that it’s hard to work this out. But the matter can be made easy—just pick a date and move on. Will Steffen, an Australian chemist, recommended starting the epoch with the beginning of the Industrial Revolution or the Atomic Age. Either way, he said, the new name would remind people of the reality: We are having an "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "undeniable",
            "ref": "v-undeniable"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " effect on our planet."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Andrew Revkin was a "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "New York Times"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " reporter. He once suggested a term similar to Anthropocene, “Anthrocene.” It never caught on, but he thinks it’s great that the problem is being discussed."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
